ationally  and the real game-changing opportunity  that has come  from our science in innovation audit is  described here this is the living  laboratory it is a funded now  opportunity  recently funded opportunity um  funded by ukri one of the  biggest grants we have ever had a  chance to be awarded and you see here  that it will operate  in the queen elizabeth university  hospital and other  related centers and  it will serve the population with  multiple  deprivation and with  polypharmacy which i'll explain in a  minute  and multimorbidity so  patients population where there is a lot  of chronic disease  chronic disease that requires multiple  drugs  which causes problems in itself  so here is a quick  description what the living laboratory  will bring  to medicine to scottish medicine and to  international medicine  and the idea is that we will implement  the pharmacogenomics based medication  management  as one of our early and important use  cases  and this will accelerate  ability to serve the nhs in scotland  ability to serve patients whilst also  giving us so much needed economic growth  creating new jobs for this deprived  area of scotland and uk  and you see here number of use cases  that are part of this big program of the  living laboratory  i mentioned pharmacogenomics based  medication management this is the key  project and i'll say a few words about  it in a minute  we will also do scottish transcript  transcriptome archive resource in short  star  we will advance imaging for precision  medicine  using ai and other modifications  and innovations we will have digital  health validation laboratory  and we will build a new building  the health innovation hub to extend  and increase all these activities  why is it important to do  pharmacogenomics  and to make sure that we control  polypharmacy polypharmacy means many  medications defined as  two or more medications and it  is particularly used in patients with  multi-morbidity who have multiple  conditions frequently patients  of age 65 and above  and this is the reason for  adverse reactions unplanned admissions  and huge additional  both suffering for patients and  increased cost  to health services all over the world  um so we  believe that the program that we  planning um on  the pharmacogenomics will be  enormously important to prevent adverse  reactions but also  to reduce the unnecessary hospital  admissions  and other adverse effects for patients  you see here that for patients over 65  years of age many many are  on multiple medications and 50 percent  of the adverse reactions that require  hospital admissions  are preventable with the methods we are  currently implementing  and to summarize this very important use  case  we have already performed some  preliminary  activity some preliminary in  silico calculations and modeling  and it showed that in this one  area of scotland um we have  the um we we have  a lot of patients who are on  polypharmacy  you see here that interestingly we  have very large cohort of patients on  three plus medications 165  000 namely that average number of  medications is much more than we thought  eight that most prevalent under  condition is high blood pressure  and most costly as we would expect  diabetes  by introducing um  pharmacogenomic way of medication  management  we could prevent  large number of adverse reactions we can  reduce the annual health  care spend very significantly with  calculated  savings of more than 85 million  pounds over three years for our system  for relatively small population one can  think that  extending this to entire country  or beyond would be a real  real real gain for the health systems  and for the population at large  so very quickly also  the additional resources that we will  produce is the scottish transcriptomes  archive resource with 30 000  mrna from clinical samples from  clinical tissues from human tissue that  would be  openly available to number of  clinical and drug discovery activities  throughout  our health system the  next use case is our digital health  validation laboratory  where we are inviting companies big and  small  to join with us to be able  to validate various innovations  such as ai  for example applied to imaging or  digital pathology and this would be done  in the middle of clinical campus with  ability to interact with clinicians and  scientists  and this is what the companies that work  with us  have asked us as the major  need that the companies have to embed  the activities in a real  clinical scenarios and last but not  least  our new health innovation hub that will  bring it all together  in a new purpose-built purpose-designed  building where we work with world-famous  developer with a special interest  in these type of clinical  science-based activities that  join academia health delivery and  industry  so a true triple helix activities  i just want to switch for last two  slides to something that is already  happening  in the same place in the same  living laboratory this is of course  testing for covet working  with colleagues from thermo fisher we  have developed  at the end of march the covet  testing laboratory and national facility  for scotland  that uses the same space and the same  living  laboratory philosophy that is triple  helix  of academia nhs in industry this  laboratory  has been one of um  great helpers to our health service  and it has currently exceeded  uh one and a half million of tests from  zero start as shown on this slide  and i just would like to acknowledge  enormous help  of our students our staff our scientists  our technicians who came and volunteered  we had more than 800 volunteers when we  started in march and we also would like  to thank colleagues from thermo fisher  who have who have been with us  throughout this journey  building completely new uh  industrial scale laboratory with  thousands of samples
 

